rot22 on OS-9 -- what was changed=================================`rot' 2.2, comp.sources.games volume 1 issue 8, 5 May 1987.  Peter da Silvawrote it; Andrew Scott Beals and Bill Randle fixed the padding and packagedthe release, and Randle's note calls it "the latest and last version".It cascades the letters of a file down the screen in a waterfall, a few at atime, until the text has fallen from the top to the bottom.  Give it a file,or pipe text in and it reads standard input.THE NAME.  The program calls itself `rot' and this disk already has one --`rot' turns a text file on its side, line one becoming column one.  Twodifferent programs, so this one keeps the archive's own name, `rot22'.ORIG/ holds the release as posted, `rot.c' and all.THREE CHANGES, all under `#ifdef OSK', so ORIG still builds where it camefrom.1.  THE TERMINAL HEADERS ARE NOT HERE.  It includes <sgtty.h>, or <termio.h>    under SYSV, and OS-9 has neither.  The only thing it wanted from them was    the line speed.2.  ospeed IS LEFT AT ZERO.  The original reads the baud rate with an ioctl    so termlib can pad its cursor motions for a slow line.  OS-9 keeps the    speed as a code in <sgstat.h>'s sg_baud rather than the number that    arithmetic wants, and on a pseudo-terminal there is no line to wait for;    zero is what termlib reads as "do not pad".  SRC/toys/screen.c does the    same -- it declares ospeed and never sets it.  On real hardware over a    genuinely slow line the waterfall will run without padding.3.  THE PAD CHARACTER IS SPELLED PC_ HERE.  It is `extern char PC' in the    source, and that is the name l68 could not resolve: the curses this    collection builds against calls it `PC_'.  A #define maps one to the    other.  ospeed, UP and BC come from the same library and needed nothing.    SRC/forum9/mshell.c uses PC_ directly for the same reason.WHAT IT NEEDS TO RUN.  TERM and TERMCAP, which SYS/login sets.  Nothing else.